TI  - Ecological dynamics and management of citrus psylla Diaphorina citri Kuwayama (Hemiptera: Psyllidae) under semi‑arid conditions in India
AU  - Pathania, Mandeep
AB  - <title>Abstract</title> <p> <italic>Diaphorina citri</italic> Kuwayama (Hemiptera: Psyllidae) is a key citrus pest in India, and an important vector of Huanglongbing. This study characterized the seasonality and population dynamics of <italic>D. citri</italic> in Kinnow mandarin orchards in south‑western Punjab in relation to weather variables and host phenology conducted between 2012-2018. Adults were present almost year‑round, surviving winter in the adult stage within the canopy, while nymphs occurred primarily during two major flushing periods: early February to April and August–September, with the first flush supporting the highest populations. Population build‑up peaked in the 13th standard meteorological week (56.36 ± 2.05 nymphs and 32.46 ± 2.49 adults per 10 cm twig). Optimal conditions for proliferation were maximum temperatures of 21.69-37.87 °C, minimum temperatures of 7.30–20.53 °C, and relative humidity of 63.90–92.66% (maximum) and 19.53–54.22% (minimum). A multiple‑regression prediction model incorporating temperature, humidity, radiation and wind speed explained 67% of the variation in <italic>D. citri</italic> populations (R² = 0.67). Among citrus cultivars, lemon, sweet lime and Kinnow mandarin were the most preferred hosts, whereas Early Gold and Mosambi were moderately infested. Chemical control trials achieved 30.72-82.90% nymph reduction 14 days after treatment, with thiamethoxam (12.6%) + lambda‑cyhalothrin (9.5%) most effective, followed by beta‑cyfluthrin (8.49%) + imidacloprid (19.81%), flonicamid, thiamethoxam and imidacloprid. Biopesticides including <italic>Beauveria bassiana</italic> (1.15%, 1×10⁸ CFU mL⁻¹), <italic>Verticillium lecanii</italic> (1.15%, 1×10⁸ CFU mL⁻¹) and horticultural mineral oil provided 47.86–52.23% reduction while remaining relatively safe to natural predators. Overall, <italic>D. citri</italic> population dynamics were strongly shaped by climatic conditions and host phenology, and both newer insecticides and biopesticides offer effective management options compatible with natural enemy conservation. </p>
