TI  - Comparative Virulence of Indigenous Entomopathogenic Fungi Against Spodoptera frugiperda Larvae on Sweet Corn
AU  - WOLDEMELAK, Wondimagegn Atilaw
AB  - <title>Abstract</title> <p> Fall armyworm (FAW), <italic>S. frugiperda</italic> is one of the destructive invasive pests of sweet corn, which causes a significant economic losses worldwide. Entomopathogenic fungi (EPF) is an alternative option to control this pest but have very specific efficacy depending upon the strains used. Thus, this study was designed to compare the efficacy of three indigenous strains of EPFs including <italic>Metarhizium anisopliae</italic> , <italic>Beauveria bassiana</italic> , and <italic>Isaria fumosorosea</italic> against third-instar FAW larvae. Sweet corn leaf disks were immersed in conidial formulation ranging from 10⁴ to 10⁸ conidia mL⁻¹, and then provided to the larvae in the Peter dish and larval mortality was recorded over 7 days. The results demonstrated that <italic>M. anisopliae</italic> was the most potent isolate, with LC₅₀ of 1.42 × 10⁵ conidia mL⁻¹, making it 4.7-fold more potent than <italic>B. bassiana</italic> and 12.3-fold more potent than <italic>I. fumosorosea</italic> . At a dosage of 1 × 10⁷ conidia mL⁻¹, <italic>M. anisopliae</italic> killed 50% of larvae in 3.8 days (LT₅₀), significantly faster than <italic>B. bassiana</italic> (5.2 days) and <italic>I. fumosorosea</italic> (7.1 days). <italic>M. anisopliae</italic> induced the highest mycosis rate (94.2%), the greatest feeding cessation index > 80% by day 3, the strongest growth inhibition (82.7%), and the most severe disruption of pupation (73.9%). All isolates met quality control standards (> 90% conidial germination; control mortality < 5%). These findings indicate that the superior performance of <italic>M. anisopliae</italic> across all virulence parameters, particularly its rapid induction of feeding cessation and high pupation disruption, provides critical sublethal benefits that extend beyond mortality reduction. In conclusion, the indigenous <italic>M. anisopliae</italic> isolate is a candidate for the biological control of <italic>S. frugiperda</italic> in sweet corn production systems. </p>
