AB  - The innovative fungus-mite collaborative control strategy based on the high resistance of predatory mites to entomopathogenic fungi offers significant advantages. However, the resistance mechanisms of predatory mites to entomopathogenic fungi remain poorly characterized. Additionally, the pathogenic and lethal risks of broad-spectrum entomopathogenic fungi to predatory mites pose constraints on their application. Therefore, analyzing the potential resistance mechanisms of predatory mites to entomopathogenic fungi is necessary to provide molecular targets for mitigating or even eliminating these side effects. Here, we investigated the role of the Toll signaling pathway in mediating N. barkeri's immune defense against B. bassiana. Expression analysis revealed that B. bassiana infection significantly upregulated six Toll pathway genes, particularly at 2 h and 24 h post-infection. RNAi targeting Toll pathway components markedly reduced mite survival rates under fungal challenge by 19.44 %-38.48 %, confirming their essential roles in resistance. Further, knockdown of Toll pathway genes also downregulated the expression of four lysozyme genes, whose promoters harbor NF-κB binding sites. This indicates that the activation of the Toll pathway and the expression of lysozyme form a tight cascade feedback pattern. Notably, these lysozyme genes were also upregulated under B. bassiana infection, and knockdown of lysozymes similarly impaired mite survival by 25.27 %-38.3 %. This study elucidates a conserved Toll-lysozyme axis as the molecular basis for N. barkeri's resistance to B. bassiana, offering potential targets for enhancing natural enemy resilience in fungus-mite collaborative control strategy.
